    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d831">

                <pb facs="0912" n="903" sameAs="frus:frus1938v05/pg903"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3331/148: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Dawson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-18T18:00:00-05:00">March 18, 1938—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Your despatch No. 39, March 4, 1938.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d831-f1"/> Since
                    it is not the practice of the Department to seek exclusive preferential
                    facilities of any nature for American trade, it does not believe that you should
                    take any action along the line indicated in your despatch under reference. There
                    is also a strong possibility that the particular type of action you suggest
                    would be held by the Treasury Department to be a bounty or grant within the
                    meaning of Section 303 of the Tariff Act of 1930,<anchor n="23" xml:id="d831-f2"
                    /> and to necessitate the imposition of equivalent countervailing duties, such
                    as were imposed upon German products as a result of roughly comparable
                    practices.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
